<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#esperanto">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esperanto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esperanto is a constructed international language created by the Polish ophthalmologist L. L. Zamenhof and first published in 1887. Designed to be easy to learn, with regular grammar and a vocabulary drawn mainly from European languages, it aimed to promote understanding between peoples. It became the most widely spoken constructed language, with a worldwide movement and its own literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esperanto.jpg" alt="Esperanto" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard H. Geoghegan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Esperanto.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before Esperanto, Johann Martin Schleyer (1831–1912) had created Volapük as a neutral universal second language. His movement gained widespread and active use in the 19th century, which showed there was demand for such a language. When the Volapük movement collapsed from within, many of its speakers joined the Esperanto movement, which was linguistically simpler and lacked central control, and many former Volapük clubs became Esperanto clubs.</p><p>L. L. Zamenhof, a Jewish ophthalmologist from Białystok, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, created Esperanto in the late 1870s and early 1880s. After several earlier versions, he self-published the first book of grammar, the Unua Libro, on 26 July 1887, under the pseudonym Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'. He called the language simply the international language. Early speakers grew fond of the pseudonym and began to use it as the name of the language. Besides his native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> and Russian, Zamenhof studied German, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, English, Spanish, Lithuanian, Italian, French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and Volapük.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The language sits between naturalistic constructed languages, which imitate existing ones, and a priori ones. About 80% of its vocabulary derives from Romance languages, and the remainder chiefly from Germanic, Greek and Slavic languages. New words are formed through extensive use of prefixes, suffixes and compounds, so a smaller set of words can express a great deal. Its default word order is subject–verb–object, and adjectives may stand before or after their nouns. One 2010 typological study concluded that Esperanto is somewhat European in character, but considerably less so than the European languages themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Universal Esperanto Association has a consultative relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, which recognised Esperanto as a medium for international understanding in 1954. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> tried to promote its teaching in member countries, but the resolutions were defeated, mainly by French delegates who saw no need for it. No country has officially adopted it. It has entered the education systems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and Eötvös Loránd University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Interlinguistics and Esperanto from 1966 to 2004.</p><p>Esperanto has an estimated 2,000 native speakers and, by two estimates, around 100,000 users. Reform proposals began with Zamenhof's own in 1894 and with Ido in 1907, and several later constructed languages were based on it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> added Esperanto to its list of intangible cultural heritage in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Esperanto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The song has been sung in many languages, among them English, Serbo-Croatian, Slovenian, German, French, Swedish, Turkish, Kurdish, Finnish, Maltese, Russian, <b>Esperanto</b>, Indonesian, Japanese, Arabic, Hindi, Punjabi, Vietnamese and Filipino.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bandiera-rossa">Bandiera Rossa</a></li><li><blockquote>After the mine was exhausted in 1885, enthusiasts promoted it as a possible <b>Esperanto</b> state called Amikejo, and the territory became known for its gambling and distillery.</blockquote><a href="/state/neutral-moresnet">Neutral Moresnet</a></li></ul></section>
